; 12-sound-editor.moritz - tutorial 12: the sound editor. A buffer~
; double-clicked opens in the sound editor, where a sound is seen (overview,
; waveform, spectrogram), selected and heard, edited with undo, drawn sample
; by sample, marked (markers, regions), processed with an object, a Musil
; script or a patch, and turned into a score. What you do there changes the
; buffer~ at once: the patch plays it as you left it.
(patch
  (obj title comment "Tutorial 12 · The sound editor: seeing a sound, editing it, processing it" (pos 20 12))
  (obj ex link "The scripts and patches of examples/14-sound-editor (a folder)" ../examples/14-sound-editor (pos 620 8))
  (obj t1 comment "The sound editor is where you look at a sound and change it by hand. A buffer~ (tutorial 3) double-clicked opens in it." (pos 20 40))
  (obj t2 comment "What you do there changes the buffer~ at once: the patch below plays it, waveform~ shows it. Turn the audio on (the toolbar)." (pos 20 58))
  (obj rd1 message "read drums.wav" (pos 20 88))
  (obj wr1 message "write ~/Documents/Moritz/t12drums.wav" (pos 140 88))
  (obj rd2 message "read Vox.wav" (pos 400 88))
  (obj crd comment "read: the sounds again, as they were" (pos 500 89))
  (obj b1 buffer~ t12drums drums.wav (pos 20 120))
  (obj b2 buffer~ t12voice Vox.wav (pos 400 120))
  (obj w1 waveform~ t12drums 360 60 (pos 20 150))
  (obj w2 waveform~ t12voice 360 60 (pos 400 150))
  (obj go1 message start (pos 20 225))
  (obj st1 message stop (pos 75 225))
  (obj go2 message start (pos 400 225))
  (obj st2 message stop (pos 455 225))
  (obj p1 play~ t12drums 2 (pos 20 255))
  (obj p2 play~ t12voice 1 (pos 400 255))
  (obj lb loadbang (pos 600 225))
  (obj on message "0.5 50" (pos 600 255))
  (obj off message "0 50" (pos 670 255))
  (obj cvol comment "volume" (pos 730 256))
  (obj ramp line~ (pos 600 285))
  (obj vl *~ (pos 20 315))
  (obj vr *~ (pos 130 315))
  (obj out dac~ (pos 20 350))
  (obj s1 comment "1. Open it. Double-click the buffer~ t12drums (the patch is locked, as it opens): the sound editor opens on the drums. Its title says buffer~ t12drums." (pos 20 395))
  (obj s1b comment "   From the top: the overview (the whole sound, the part shown below framed), the lane of regions, the lane of markers, a waveform a channel, the spectrogram." (pos 20 413))
  (obj s2 comment "2. The views. Wave, Spectrum and Both, in its toolbar, choose what is drawn. Cmd+ and Cmd- zoom (or Cmd and the wheel, or a pinch); Fit shows it all again;" (pos 20 445))
  (obj s2b comment "   the wheel, or a drag in the overview, scrolls. In the spectrogram time goes right and frequency up: a drum hit is a stripe from bottom to top (every" (pos 20 463))
  (obj s2c comment "   frequency at once). Double-click the voice too: its vowels are stacks of horizontal lines, its harmonics; its consonants are noise, a blur across all the heights." (pos 20 481))
  (obj s3 comment "3. Select and listen. Drag across the sound to select a part (Shift-click extends it, Cmd+A takes it all); a click alone puts the playhead there." (pos 20 513))
  (obj s3b comment "   Space plays the selection, or from the playhead; Space again stops. Cmd+L (the loop button) repeats the selection: select one beat of the drums and loop it." (pos 20 531))
  (obj s3c comment "   The editor plays through the audio output beside the patches: click start above at the same time, and you hear both." (pos 20 549))
  (obj s4 comment "4. Edit. Select the second hit of the drums and choose Edit > Reverse (Edit is also the right button on the sound): the hit backwards. Click start above:" (pos 20 581))
  (obj s4b comment "   the patch plays the drums as you edited them, and waveform~ shows them. Try Fade In, Fade Out, Silence, Gain..., Normalize, Cut, Copy and Paste, Trim to Selection." (pos 20 599))
  (obj s4c comment "   Each edit is one step of Undo: Cmd+Z takes it back, Shift+Cmd+Z does it again. read drums.wav (at the top) brings the sound back as it was." (pos 20 617))
  (obj s5 comment "5. The pencil. Choose Draw and zoom in until each sample has a pixel (until then the status line asks you to): drag over the waveform to move the samples." (pos 20 649))
  (obj s5b comment "   Draw a sharp spike into a quiet part and listen: a click. Smooth the start of a hit into a gentle curve: it loses its attack. A stroke is one step of Undo." (pos 20 667))
  (obj s6 comment "6. Markers mark moments. M puts one at the playhead; with the Marker tool a click puts one. Drag one along its lane; right-click it to rename or delete it." (pos 20 699))
  (obj s6b comment "   Markers > Markers from Onsets marks where each hit begins; Markers from Beats marks the pulse (the status line says the tempo); Markers from Novelty marks" (pos 20 717))
  (obj s6c comment "   where the sound changes character (try it on the voice). Markers > Clear Markers takes them all out; like every edit, Undo takes a marker back." (pos 20 735))
  (obj s7 comment "7. Regions are named parts of the sound. Select a part, then Markers > Add a Region from the Selection (or Add, in the regions panel under the sound)." (pos 20 767))
  (obj s7b comment "   Regions from Onsets cuts the drums between their onsets, a region a hit; Regions from Types (soundtypes) colours each piece by its type: alike hits, alike colours." (pos 20 785))
  (obj s7c comment "   The regions table lists them (name, start, end, type, energy, pitch): a click selects a region, so Space plays it; a double-click renames it." (pos 20 803))
  (obj s8 comment "8. Process with an object. Select one hit, then Process > pvoc: stretch or transpose...: Stretch 4, Process: the hit four times longer, at the same pitch." (pos 20 835))
  (obj s8b comment "   Now the voice: select a phrase, pvoc again with Transpose -7, Formants keep, then (Undo) with move. Kept, a voice stays a voice; moved, it sounds like a larger body." (pos 20 853))
  (obj s8c comment "   With nothing selected the whole sound is processed; Esc stops a long one. The Process menu's Result: replace the selection, or a new sound in a window of its own." (pos 20 871))
  (obj s8d comment "   The other objects there: pursuit, typematch, hpss, nmf, sparkle~'s cross-synthesis, filterdesign's filters, condec (tutorials 5, 8 and 10)." (pos 20 889))
  (obj s9 comment "9. Process with a Musil script: a .mu file that defines (process x sr), x a list of channels, sr the rate, and returns the new sound. File > New Code, then type:" (pos 20 921))
  (obj s9b comment "        function process (x sr) {" (pos 20 941))
  (obj s9c comment "            var t (/ (range (length (head x))) sr)" (pos 20 957))
  (obj s9d comment "            return (map x (function (c) (* c (- 1 (* 0.4 (+ 1 (sin (* tau 6 t))))))))" (pos 20 973))
  (obj s9e comment "        }" (pos 20 989))
  (obj s9f comment "   Save it as tremolo.mu in Documents/Moritz. Select a phrase of the voice, Process > A Musil script (process x sr)..., choose tremolo.mu: the voice trembles" (pos 20 1009))
  (obj s9g comment "   six times a second. Change the 6 (or the 0.4, the depth), save, and run it again: Process lists it under Recent. Process > Examples has those of" (pos 20 1027))
  (obj s9h comment "   examples/14-sound-editor ready to run (stutter, reverse-echo, octave-down, widen, a comb, a hall); the link at the top opens their folder to read them." (pos 20 1045))
  (obj s10 comment "10. Process with a patch: a .moritz file whose inlet~ takes the sound in and whose outlet~ gives it back. File > New, then three boxes, connected in a column:" (pos 20 1077))
  (obj s10b comment "   inlet~, reverb~ 0.4 3, outlet~. Save it as hall.moritz in Documents/Moritz. Select a word of the voice, Process > A patch (inlet~ to outlet~)..., choose it," (pos 20 1095))
  (obj s10c comment "   Tail 3 seconds: the word in a hall. The tail rings on over what follows the word, mixed with it (silence at its end is left out)." (pos 20 1113))
  (obj s10d comment "   A stereo sound goes into two inlet~s, left and right. The folder of examples/14-sound-editor has hall, comb-resonator and ring-modulator." (pos 20 1131))
  (obj s11 comment "11. From regions to a score. Make regions in the drums (step 7), then To score in the regions panel (or Markers > To Score...): a .score file, a sound event a region." (pos 20 1163))
  (obj s11b comment "   Save it as t12hits.score in Documents/Moritz, then click read below: the score box shows the hits on a line of sounds, and play plays them, each at its place." (pos 20 1181))
  (obj s11c comment "   To coll... writes the same regions as rows (start end type energy pitch name), Musil data a text editor reads. Scores are the subject of tutorial 13." (pos 20 1199))
  (obj rs message "read ~/Documents/Moritz/t12hits.score" (pos 20 1230))
  (obj sp message play (pos 280 1230))
  (obj ss message stop (pos 325 1230))
  (obj sl message "locate 1" (pos 370 1230))
  (obj sc score t12hits @size 560 110 (pos 20 1260))
  (obj sv1 *~ 0.6 (pos 20 1400))
  (obj sv2 *~ 0.6 (pos 130 1400))
  (obj sout dac~ (pos 20 1435))
  (obj s12 comment "12. Saving. A buffer~ is the patch's memory: its edits and markers stay while the patch is open, and nothing is written to the disk. In its sound editor, File > Save" (pos 20 1475))
  (obj s12b comment "   writes a copy as a WAV file where you choose (its markers beside it, in name.wav.markers); write, at the top, writes the sound alone from the patch." (pos 20 1493))
  (obj s12c comment "   File > Open Sound... edits a sound file itself: Save writes the file back, and closing asks when it has edits. Open t12drums.wav, change it, close it: it asks." (pos 20 1511))
  (obj next comment "Next: tutorial 13, scores. Examples: 14 sound editor (Help > Browser); and 16 timeline's regions, sounds from the editor arranged." (pos 20 1545))
  (connect rd1 0 b1 0)
  (connect wr1 0 b1 0)
  (connect rd2 0 b2 0)
  (connect go1 0 p1 0)
  (connect st1 0 p1 0)
  (connect go2 0 p2 0)
  (connect st2 0 p2 0)
  (connect p1 0 vl 0)
  (connect p1 1 vr 0)
  (connect p2 0 vl 0)
  (connect p2 0 vr 0)
  (connect lb 0 on 0)
  (connect on 0 ramp 0)
  (connect off 0 ramp 0)
  (connect ramp 0 vl 1)
  (connect ramp 0 vr 1)
  (connect vl 0 out 0)
  (connect vr 0 out 1)
  (connect rs 0 sc 0)
  (connect sp 0 sc 0)
  (connect ss 0 sc 0)
  (connect sl 0 sc 0)
  (connect sc 0 sv1 0)
  (connect sc 1 sv2 0)
  (connect sv1 0 sout 0)
  (connect sv2 0 sout 1))
